#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# unevaluated

#% text
# `unevaluated(e)` passes `e` itself, unevaluated, as an argument of a call.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Written as a strict argument of a call `f(..., unevaluated(e), ...)`, the call receives `e` as it is written, not its value; the call's own definitions and natives then see the unevaluated term.

#% item
# If no definition of `f` applies, the result keeps the wrapper: `f(unevaluated(e))`.

#% item
# In an argument the function holds, `unevaluated(e)` stays as it is; `eval(unevaluated(e))` keeps the wrapper too.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# `length` sees the four terms of the sum, not its value:

#% cell
length(unevaluated(5 + 6 + 7 + 8))
#% output
#= 4

#% cell
rule f(x_) := g(x)
f(unevaluated(1 + 1))
#% output
#= g(2)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Without a definition that applies, the wrapper stays:

#% cell
h(unevaluated(1 + 2))
#% output
#= h(unevaluated(1 + 2))

#% cell
hold(eval(unevaluated(1 + 2)))
#% output
#= hold(unevaluated(1 + 2))

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `eval`, `set_attributes`
